<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
  <section xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-02-growing-area">
    <title>New Building Block: Growing Area (2D)</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>
        With farming in mind, one of the major parts of farming is
        waiting for plants to grow. In addition, the longer you wait, 
        the bigger the plants get, and, in many cases, if you wait longer, 
        you get more from your crop. In order to simulate the stages 
        of plant growth, we want the growing area to have a way to change
        its appearance after different amounts of time. We also want the 
        score, and possibly the energy gained, to be different at the same
        stages. To make collection a game of skill, we want the collecting 
        tool to simulate a lasso, where the lasso will grow to the right size
        and only collect when it is in a small range close to the ideal size.
        For this when the ship launches the lasso, we want it to grow from a 
        small size up to a size that is a little bigger than the ideal size 
        before the lasso breaks apart and dissappears, but if it is able to
        harvest a crystal, then the crystal is collected from the space.
    </p>
    <p>
        Since there are 2 different elements that will grow, we look at the
        similarities between the elements to see if we can make a common parent
        class for them. Then the parent script can hold the common elements and
        perform the common actions instead of needing to write the same behavior 
        twice. We can then use the subclass to differentiate the behavior, such 
        as how it looks, the fact that the crystals are stationary and the lassos
        will move, and that lassos will disapear after they get to a certain size,
        while the crystals will grow to a maximum size and only leave the space 
        if they are collected. Lassos also are sourced from the location of the 
        ship, while the crystals can grow anywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
        The different stages of growth for crystals and the specific sizes of the
        lasso for collection and disappearing can both be handled by a number of 
        thresholds. Since there are multiple thresholds, this could be handled by
        an array or multiple individual variables. For flexibility in the number
        of thresholds, an array can prevent the creation of values that won't be 
        used at the expense of the individual values needing an ad hoc naming scheme
        or relying on the behavior of the code to determine what each threshold 
        represents.  
    </p>
    <p> This section will go over the base growing area scene and its accompanying script
        followed by the collector (for the lasso) and prize (for the crystal) scenes
        along with the script changes needed for each of them.
    </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-growing-area-scene">
      <title>The Growing Area Scene</title>
      <introduction>
      <p>
        To make the Growing Area Scene, all that we need is a single 
        <c>Area2D</c> Node as we see in 
        <xref ref="space-farm-class-hierarchy" text="type-global-title"/>.
        Then we can attach an empty script with
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsubsection>
        <title>Growing Area Member Variables</title>
        <p>
            The first set of member variables of concern are related to the
            size of the area. When determining size, it is natural to think 
            of changing the size values of a Node, but the <c>Node2D</c> class
            doesn't have an intrinsic size. It just has a <c>Vector2</c> that scales
            the size of its parts. If the aspect ratio is fixed, then the scale
            for x and y should be the same. Since we don't have direct access to 
            the size, the Growing Area has the benefit of abstraction where the 
            scale can be used directly to determine growth rather than computing 
            the size each time when applying growth.
        </p>
        <p>
            So, all of the growth variables are in terms of scale, and there are 
            three fixed values that are necessary for determining growth. The first
            is the starting scale, the second is the maximum scale, and the third is
            the growth rate (how much the scale changes per second). Godot has 
            a <c>Curve</c> class for allowing a rate to change smoothly over time, 
            so in addition to the 3 fixed parameters we will have an optional growth curve.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
var start_scale: float = 0.1
var max_scale: float = 0.9
var growth_rate: float = 0.03          # scale units per second
@export var growth_curve: Curve        # optional easing; sampled 0-1 by (current/max)
        </program>
        <p>
            In addition to the variables related to how much to grow, there are also
            decisions about when growth starts, and what happens when growth stops.
            We will use the <c>auto_start</c> member variable to determine if growth 
            starts as soon as the growing area is in the scene. We use <c>destroy_past_max</c>
            to determinine whether the growing area should free itself when it gets
            to the maximum size.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
var auto_start: bool = true
var destroy_past_max: bool = false
        </program>
        <p>
            The above mentioned member variables will typically be fixed after 
            instantiation of the member. However, there are a couple of member 
            variables that will change over the lifetime of the <c>Node</c>. These 
            are the current scale, and whether the area is currently growing.
            There is already a <c>scale</c> value that reflects the current scale,
            but since <c>scale</c> is a <c>Vector2</c>, we want to have a one diminsional
            value that represents the overall scale where there is no room for ambiguity by
            having 2 placeholders for the value. So, <c>current_scale</c> and all of the 
            above fixed scale values are of type <c>float</c>, and not <c>Vector2</c>. 
            This simplifies assignment, but requires that the <c>scale</c> value on the
            growing area be determined by multiplying the <c>current_scale</c> by 
            <c>Vector2.ONE</c> to convert it to a <c>Vector2</c>. 
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
var current_scale: float
var _growing: bool = false
        </program>
        <p>
            There are 3 more parts of the growing area, that aren't directly used
            by the growing area. Either they are used by potential observers of the 
            areas or they are used by the subclasses of a growing area. The first is
            the array of scale thresholds. This array is by default an empty array, but
            can be overriden in a subclass to be values that make sense for its particular purpose.
            The other 2 are signals. There is one signal to specify that growth occured and a 
            second signal to specify that the maximum growth has been reached. These signals could
            be used to determine sound or visual effects that originate from another source such as
            the environment.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
var thresholds: Array[float] = []
signal max_size_reached(source: GrowingArea)
signal size_changed(scale: float)
        </program>
        <p>The next section will describe how the member variables are 
            initialized and processed by default.
        </p>
    </subsubsection>
    <subsubsection>
        <title>Growing Area Methods</title>
        <p>
            The <c>_ready()</c> method sets the scale to the <c>start_scale</c>,
            updates the <c>current_scale</c> to the <c>start_scale</c>, and
            starts the growth.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
# Called when the node enters the scene tree for the first time.
func _ready() -> void:
    current_scale = start_scale
    scale = Vector2.ONE * current_scale
    if auto_start:
        start_growth()
        </program>
        <p>
            The <c>_growing</c> parameter has 2 methods for starting and stopping growth.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func start_growth() -> void:
    _growing = true

func stop_growth() -> void:
    _growing = false
        </program>
        <p>
            The <c>_process()</c> method runs the growth each step based on
            the member variables. If <c>_growing</c> is false, then none of 
            the code in <c>_process()</c> should happen since it's only related to the 
            growth.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    if not _growing:
        return
</program>
        <p>
            A local variable <c>step</c> is used to determine how much the scale
            will change by. The initial step size is based on the growth rate times
            delta, and then if there is a growth curve, the curve is sampled based
            on the ratio of current_scale to max_scale.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func _process(delta: float) -> void:
     
    # ... after earlier code

    var step: float = growth_rate * delta
    if growth_curve:
        step *= growth_curve.sample(clamp(current_scale / max_scale, 0.0, 1.0))
</program>
        <p>
            The <c>current_scale</c> is the increased by the step size
            but is not allowed to go above the max_scale, and when max
            scale is reached the growth is stopped, a signal is sent,
            and when specified, the node is removed from the scene.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func _process(delta: float) -> void:
     
    # ... after earlier code

    current_scale += step

    if current_scale >= max_scale:
        current_scale = max_scale
        stop_growth()
        emit_signal("max_size_reached",self)
        if destroy_past_max:
            queue_free()
            return
        </program>
        <p>
            The <c>_apply_growth()</c> method is called so that
            the scale can be applied appropriately to the child nodes
            of the child scenes by overriding the method. And then the
            size changed signal is emitted so that it can be observed 
            by the environment.
        </p>
<program language="gdscript">
<code>
func _process(delta: float) -> void:
     
    # ... after earlier code

    _apply_growth()
    emit_signal("size_changed", current_scale)
</code>
        </program>
        <p>
            That ends the <c>_process()</c> method.
            <c>_apply_growth()</c> just updates the scale
            member variable. 
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func _apply_growth() -> void:
    scale = Vector2.ONE * current_scale
        </program>
        <p>
            That completes the <c>growing_area.gd</c> script, but a scene that uses
            growing area is needed to see an example.
        </p>
    </subsubsection>
    </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-collector-scene">
      <title>Lasso: The Collector Scene</title>
      <introduction>
      <p>
        The first <c>GrowingArea</c> that we will build is the lasso, 
        our <c>Collector</c> scene. We call it a collector because it 
        collects prizes. The collector will have a GrowingArea as a root
        Node, and then a CollisionShape2D to detect collissions, and 
        a Node2D that we'll rename to RingVisual to draw the growing 
        lasso. <xref ref="space-farm-class-hierarchy" text="type-global-title"/>
        has a picture of the scene structure for the collector.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsubsection>
        <title>Scene Setup</title>
        <p>
            The CollectionShape2D should be set up with a CircleArea Shape
            with a Radius of 10 pixels. The RingVisual Node2D should get it's own
            script as described below.
        </p>
    </subsubsection>
<subsubsection>
        <title>RingVisual Scene and Script</title>
        <p>
            The RingVisual Scene is a Node2D that is just their so the
            <c>_draw()</c> method can be overriden to use the canvas to 
            draw a light blue (or a color of your choice) ring.
        </p>
        <p> The member variables related to the ring are
            <c>band_width</c>, <c>band_color</c>, 
            <c>point_count</c>, and for the size at scale 1.0,
            <c>scale_one_radius</c>.  Here is how the script should 
            start.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
extends Node2D
class_name RingVisual

var band_width: float = 4.0
var band_color: Color = Color(0.1, 0.8, 1.0, 0.9)
var point_count: int = 64

const scale_one_radius: float = 150.0
        </program>
        <p> 
            A final member variable is the radius, and when the
            radius changed, we want to redraw, so we add a special 
            setter method to make sure that any time the radius changes
            the visual is updated.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
var radius: float = 0.0:
    set(value):
        radius = value
        queue_redraw()
        </program>
        <p> 
            The <c>_draw()</c> function can now use the member variables to
            draw an arc that has a fixed width to draw the ring that represents 
            the lasso of the collector. The following draw function should be added
            to the RingVisual script.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func _draw() -> void:
    if radius &lt;= 0.0:
        return
    draw_arc(Vector2.ZERO, radius, 0.0, TAU, point_count, band_color, band_width, true)
        </program>
        <p> 
            Finally, as the lasso grows, the scale of the ring needs to be
            updated as follows:
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func update_scale(scale: float) -> void:
    radius = scale_one_radius * scale
        </program>
        <p> 
            Now that this script is complete, we will move on to
            the parent node's script.
        </p>
    </subsubsection>
        <subsubsection>
        <title>Collector Script</title>
        <p>
            After adding the GrowingArea, and renaming it Collector,
            make a new script to attach to it. The Collector, has four
            member variables, a speed, a velocity, a reference to the 
            current view_size, and a min_collection_distance, which 
            determines how close the center of the collector needs to be 
            from the center of the prize in order for it to be collected.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
extends GrowingArea
class_name Collector

var speed = 350
var velocity: Vector2
var view_size: Vector2i
var min_collect_distance: float = 100.0
        </program>
        <p>
            Since the default values for collectors is
            different than the default values for <c>growing_area.gd</c>
            the initial <c>auto_start</c> value false, and the
            <c>start_scale</c> will be 0.07. 
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func _init() -> void:
    auto_start = false
    start_scale = 0.07
        </program>
        <p>
            <c>_ready()</c> can set the other values such as 
            the growth rate and the size thresholds. The first 2
            size thresholds are the minimum and maximum collection
            radius, and the final threshold is 0.01 less than the 
            maximum scale. Destroy past max is true because we want
            the lasso to stop existing a little bit after it's collection
            zone. The view size is set because we want the lasso to 
            wrap the screen just like the player will.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
# Called when the node enters the scene tree for the first time.
func _ready() -> void:
    super._ready()
    growth_rate = .4
    thresholds = [.175,.185,.195]
    max_scale = thresholds[2] + 0.01
    destroy_past_max = true
    view_size = get_viewport().get_visible_rect().size
        </program>
        <p>
            The lasso's location will start from the location that it's launched
            from, and the start method will accept that location and direction so that it 
            is launched from the front of the ship and go the direction that the 
            ship is pointed.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func start(_transform: Transform2D) -> void:
    transform = _transform
    velocity = transform.x * speed
    start_growth()
        </program>
        <p>
            The lasso's has only a small size range where it can collect 
            the crystal. This is indicated in the first two threshold values. 
            In addition, there is a maximum distance between the center of the
            lasso and the center of the crystal being collected in order for 
            collection to occur. This is expressed in the <c>can_collect</c> 
            method.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func can_collect(prize_position: Vector2) -> bool:
    return (thresholds[0] &lt;= current_scale and 
            current_scale &lt;= thresholds[1] and 
            prize_position.distance_to(position) &lt; min_collect_distance)
        </program>
        <p>
            <c>_apply_growth()</c> is overriden for collector so that
            the ring drawing gets bigger, but the scale is not changed
            so that the collision area isn't changed either.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func _apply_growth() -> void:
    $RingVisual.update_scale(current_scale)
        </program>
        <p>
            For the collector, in addition to growing, <c>_process()</c>
            needs to update the location based on velocity in addition to
            updating the size. So after calling super.process(), 
            that's what is done.
        </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
func process(delta: float) -> void:
    super.process(delta)
    # wrapping code
    if position.x > view_size.x:
        position.x = 0
    if position.x &lt; 0:
        position.x = view_size.x
    if position.y > view_size.y:
        position.y = 0
    if position.y &lt; 0:
        position.y = view_size.y

    # update position
    position += velocity * delta
        </program>
    </subsubsection>
    <subsubsection>
        <title>Initial Testing</title>
        <p>
            Now the script is ready. To test it on it's own,
            add the following code to the end of <c>_ready()</c>
            to see the lasso moving across the view diagonally down 
            and to the right. Run the Collector scene. 
            Then change any member values that 
            you'd like to change the size, speed, color, or any
            other part that you want to adjust to your liking.
        </p>
        
        <program language="gdscript">
start((Transform2D.IDENTITY).rotated(PI/4))
        </program>
        <p>
            Once everything is adjusted. Remove the code, so 
            that the ship can launch the lasso correctly.
        </p>
    </subsubsection>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-prize-scene">
      <title>Energy Crystals: The Prize Scene</title>
      <introduction>
      <p>
        Next we'll work our our Prize, the energy crystals. The crystals 
        are stationary, they need a collision area, and they use a Sprite2D 
        for the visual representation. <xref ref="space-farm-class-hierarchy" text="type-global-title"/>.
        shows the Prize scene on the lower right. The Prize is a GrowingArea, and it has two children, 
        CollisionShape2D and Sprite2D.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsubsection>
        <title>Prize Scene Setup</title>
    <p>
        Like the Collector, The root node for the Prize scene should be a GrowingArea.
        Then add a CollisionShape2D and a Sprite2D. On the Sprite2D, you 
        can drag one of the 3 crystals from the art folder as a placeholder.
        Since, the picture will change based on the size threshold. It's not 
        necessary to have an image, but it helps for the next step of
        sizing the CircleShape2D around the image of the crystal. If you 
        decide to scale the Sprite2D, make sure you do that before sizing the
        collision shape. If not, then the collision won't match the image.
    </p>
</subsubsection>
<subsubsection>
        <title>Prize Script</title>
    <p>
        Attach a script to the Prize node. Name it Prize and
        create 3 member variables for the texture of the 
        Sprite2D.
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
extends GrowingArea
class_name Prize

var small: Texture2D = load("res://art/small-prize.png")
var med: Texture2D = load("res://art/med-prize.png")
var large: Texture2D = load("res://art/large-prize.png")    
    </program>
    <p>
        <!-- TODO move signals to basic interaction -->
        Then create 2 signals. One for when the prize is
        collected with a value for the score/energy for collecting,
        and the other for when the prize is hit by the 
        ship, and the amount of damage that the ship takes.
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
signal collected(value: int)
signal hit(value: int)
    </program>
    <!-- TODO finishe move signals-->
    <p>
        Then there should be a member variable that keeps track
        of how much energy the crystal is holding.
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
var value: int = 0
    </program>
    <!--TODO: this should be part of basic interaction-->
    <p>
        The last member variable is an Array of the Nodes that
        the prize is currently touching.
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
var _touching_collectors: Array[Node] = []
    </program>
    <!-- TODO: end basic interaction-->
    <p>
        The ready process will call super's ready and then
        set the two scale thresholds to determine when to change from small to medium,
        and when to change from medium to large.
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
func _ready() -> void:
    super._ready()
    thresholds = [.3,.6]
    </program>
    <p>
        process will use the thresholds to both determine the image used
        when at the size and the value of energy/damage when at that size.
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
# Called every frame. 'delta' is the elapsed time since the previous frame.
func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    super._process(delta)
    if current_scale &lt; thresholds[0]:
        $Sprite2D.texture = small
        value = 10
    elif current_scale &lt; thresholds[1]:
        $Sprite2D.texture = med
        value = 25
    else:
        $Sprite2D.texture = large
        value = 50
    <!-- TODO move next line to basic interaction-->
    check_touching_collectors()
    </program>

    <p>
        The check_touching_collectors function loops 
        through the touching_collectors array and 
        checks if any of them are able to collect based 
        on the collection constraints. And if they are
        then the prize is removed from the scene and
        the collected energy is emitted.
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
func check_touching_collectors() -> void:
    for area in _touching_collectors:
        var collector: Collector = area as Collector
        if collector.can_collect(self.global_position):
            emit_signal("collected",value)
            queue_free()
    </program>
    <p>
        There are two companion functions that put the collectors
        into the touching_collectors array. These are _on_area_entered
        which uses the collisionarea2D's area entered signal,
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
func _on_area_entered(area: Area2D) -> void:
    if area.is_in_group("collector"):
        if area in _touching_collectors:
            return
        _touching_collectors.append(area)

    </program>
    <p>
        and _on_area_exited, which uses the CollisionArea2D's area_exited
        signal,
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
func _on_area_exited(area: Area2D) -> void:
    if area.is_in_group("collector"):
        if area in _touching_collectors:
            _touching_collectors.erase(area)
    </program>
    <p> 
        Since the ship is a RigidBody2D, the body_entered
        signal is used for when the ship makes contact with
        a prize and both destroys the prize and damages the ship
        using the hit signal.
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
func _on_body_entered(body: Node2D) -> void:
    if body.is_in_group("player"):
        emit_signal("hit",value)
        queue_free()
    </program>
    <!-- TODO end move last part of basic interaction-->
    <p>
        That concludes the visual part of the Prize script.
    </p>
</subsubsection>
<subsubsection>
        <title>Initial Testing</title>
    <p>
        Run the prize scene and watch the crystals grow.
    </p>
</subsubsection>
      </subsection>
    </section>
